<Doc title="DFPlayer Mini" subtitle="serial MP3 player, DIP-16" id="dfplayerMini">

<Include file="pinouts/dfplayer-mini.bdoc"/>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>module</Cell><Cell>Pico</Cell><Cell>wiring</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="power">VCC</Cell><Cell class="power">VBUS</Cell><Cell>5 V, 100 uF across VCC/GND</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="ground">GND</Cell><Cell class="ground">GND</Cell><Cell>common, even on a separate supply</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">RX</Cell><Cell class="serial">GP14</Cell><Cell>1 kOhm in series; GP14 is UART0 TX</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">TX</Cell><Cell class="serial">GP15</Cell><Cell>direct; GP15 is UART0 RX</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="digital">BUSY</Cell><Cell class="digital">GP9</Cell><Cell>LOW while playing; pulled up in firmware</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>SPK_1 / SPK_2</Cell><Cell>-</Cell><Cell>speaker, neither terminal grounded</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Note><Bold>Played on 2026-08-31</Bold> at 9600 baud,
<Code>mp3/0001.mp3</Code> through <Code>SOUND PLAY 1</Code>. BUSY on GP9 reads
<Code>busy=yes</Code> while the track sounds.</Note>

<Warn><Bold>GP14 and GP15 need GPIO_FUNC_UART_AUX, not GPIO_FUNC_UART.</Bold>
On RP2350 funcsel 2 on this pair is UART0 CTS and RTS - flow control. The data
lines are funcsel 0x0b. With the wrong one the port opens, the baud rate sets,
every write returns normally and not one byte leaves the chip. Handled once in
<Code>uart::open</Code>.</Warn>

<Warn><Bold>The directions are fixed by the silicon.</Bold> GP14 is UART0_TX and
GP15 is UART0_RX, so the 1 kOhm goes into the module's RX from GP14. Reversed,
two outputs face each other and two inputs face each other - nothing is damaged
and nothing happens.</Warn>

<Warn><Bold>Never drive SPK1/SPK2 and DAC_L/DAC_R at the same time.</Bold>
SPK1/SPK2 is a bridged amplifier and DAC_L/DAC_R is a line level tap; driving
both is a fight the amplifier wins and the module loses.</Warn>

<Warn><Bold>The silkscreen wins over this or any table.</Bold> Pins 6 and 8 read
<Code>SPK_1</Code> then <Code>SPK_2</Code> downward; the DFRobot table has
<Code>SPK2</Code> on 6 and <Code>SPK1</Code> on 8. Harmless either way - the
output is differential, so a swap only inverts phase. Pin 5 read as
<Code>DAC_I</Code> is <Code>DAC_L</Code>, the left channel.</Warn>

<Note><Bold>The card takes 1.5 - 3 s to mount.</Bold> A play sent before that is
discarded with no error and no sound, which is the same symptom as a swapped
wire, a dead speaker or a brownout. <Code>SOUND RESET</Code> pays the wait; boot
does not.</Note>

<Note><Bold>If it resets mid-track, that is the supply.</Bold> The amplifier is
class-D and its current follows the audio, so on VBUS - laptop USB through the
Pico's polyfuse - a loud passage can drop the rail and reboot the board. It
reads exactly like a firmware crash.</Note>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>Pico pins</Cell><Cell>already taken</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="digital">GP0/GP1</Cell><Cell>servo, ESC</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">GP4/GP5</Cell><Cell>I2C</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="digital">GP10-GP13</Cell><Cell>lamps, on borrowed ToF XSHUT pins</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">GP14/GP15</Cell><Cell>this module; were the tail lamps</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">GP16-GP19</Cell><Cell>SD header</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Note>GP14/GP15 are the only pads carrying UART0 that cost neither GP0/GP1 nor
GP12/GP13, which is why the tail lamps gave them up. The permanent lamp move -
<Code>GP2/GP3</Code> indicators, <Code>GP6/GP7</Code> tails, <Code>GP8</Code>
heads - frees GP10 through GP13 and gives the tails a home again.
<Code>lib/pins.hxx</Code> is the map, and it static_asserts that no two
subsystems claim one pad.</Note>

</Doc>
